<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yellow River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yellow River is the second-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, flowing from the Tibetan plateau across the north China plain to the Bohai Sea. Chinese civilisation is considered to have begun along it, but it is notorious for devastating floods and changes of course. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qapaghan-qaghan">Qapaghan Qaghan</a> crossed it repeatedly in raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, and its floods of 1344 contributed to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="Yellow River" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: fading · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_River_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Yellow River, or Huang He, is the second-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the sixth-longest in the world, an estimated 5,464 kilometres long with a drainage basin of 795,000 square kilometres. It rises in the Bayan Har Mountains and flows generally eastwards, makes the 1,500-kilometre Ordos Loop through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, turns south to form the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, turns east again at its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> and crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> to the Bohai Sea. It is named for the yellow colour of its water, from the sediment it carries from the Loess Plateau. Chronicles indicate that the river, once simply called the river, became muddy sometime between 367 BCE and 165 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The basin was the birthplace of ancient Chinese civilisation. According to traditional historiography the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xia-dynasty">Xia dynasty</a> originated on its banks around 2100 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Shiji records that the Xia were founded after the tribes around the river united to fight its floods. One estimate counts 1,593 floods in the 2,540 years between 595 BCE and 1946, and the river has been nicknamed both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s Pride and China's Sorrow.</p><p>In 602 BCE it shifted several hundred kilometres east at Suxu Mouth, the first major course change recorded in history. In 1128 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> troops under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> governor Du Chong breached the southern dikes to stop the advancing Jin army, and the river shifted south of the Shandong Peninsula into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a>, where it flowed until 1855, merging with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>. Between 1851 and 1855 it returned north, amid floods that provoked the Nien and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellions</a>, and it more or less adopted its present course in the flood of 1897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Floods of the river have killed over one million people in all. Among the deadliest were the flood of 1344 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, the flood of 1887, which killed between 900,000 and 2 million people, and the flood of 1931, which killed an estimated 1 to 4 million and is the worst recorded flood by deaths. Flooding in 1898 left whole communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> destitute and, with the drought that followed, contributed to the rise of the Boxer movement. About 120 million people live in the basin, which holds 13 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s cultivated land.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yellow River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zu Ti recovered much of the land south of the <b>Yellow River</b> in the 310s, and Huan Wen, the dominant general of the mid-century, conquered the Cheng Han state in Sichuan in 347, reached the outskirts of Chang'an against the Former Qin in 354 and retook Luoyang in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>North China was a rich agricultural region, but war, land seizures and the <b>Yellow River</b>, which broke its banks and changed course in 1194, brought hardship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1004 the Empress Dowager Chengtian and Emperor Shengzong led an army deep into Song territory to Chanyuan, near the <b>Yellow River</b>, forcing the treaty that brought more than a century of peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the failed invasion of Northern Wei in 450, the provinces south of the <b>Yellow River</b> were devastated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongol-led Yuan had been weakened by internal strife, fiscal mismanagement, devastating <b>Yellow River</b> floods and widespread uprisings, and regional warlords and rebel leaders seized power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 422–423 Emperor Mingyuan took Luoyang, Xuchang and Hulao and most of Henan and western Shandong; Emperor Wen's great invasion of 450 was lured across the <b>Yellow River</b>, flanked and destroyed, and Wei armies laid waste the provinces to the south before withdrawing in January 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li></ul></section>
